 this diagnosis. Additionally, the X-ray findings of narrowing of the acromiohumeral space, sclerosis, and spurring of the acromion further confirm the presence of chronic impingement and degenerative changes. The positive rheumatoid factor indicates the presence of rheumatoid arthritis, which can contribute to the shoulder pathology by causing inflammation and joint damage. Given the patient's history of amateur tennis play, repetitive overhead activities can exacerbate rotator cuff injuries and impingement.\n\nHowever, the most likely primary diagnosis based on the clinical presentation and imaging findings is **Osteoarthritis** (Option D). Osteoarthritis is a degenerative joint disease that can cause narrowing of the acromiohumeral space, sclerosis, and spurring of the acromion, which are consistent with the X-ray findings. While rheumatoid arthritis (Option C) can contribute to joint damage, the clinical presentation and imaging findings are more indicative of osteoarthritis.\n\nIn the emergency setting, management should focus on pain control and referral to appropriate specialists for further evaluation and treatment. Pain control can be achieved with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) and possibly corticosteroid injections. Referral to a rheumatologist and an orthopedic surgeon is recommended for long-term management and to prevent further joint damage. Additionally, a referral to a physical therapist specializing in sports injuries can be beneficial to address the underlying biomechanical issues and to develop a rehabilitation program that includes strengthening exercises for the rotator cuff and scapular stabilizers, as well as flexibility exercises to improve shoulder range of motion. This multidisciplinary approach will help manage the patient's symptoms and improve his functional capacity, allowing him to continue his tennis activities with reduced risk of further injury. The patient should also be advised to avoid activities that exacerbate 